TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup structural roles",
    "organizer role",
    "shaper role",
    "terminal role",
    "lineup composition",
    "player talent",
    "talent control",
    "within-team variation"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "definition and assignment of structural roles",
    "measuring lineup coverage",
    "controlling for player talent in lineup analysis",
    "demonstrating separability of role effects from talent",
    "quantifying within-team variation of lineup composition",
    "validating talent control mechanisms"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available, so nothing is established by prior work."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available, so everything remains open, including definitions of roles, methods for measuring coverage, techniques for separating role effects from talent, and how to validate talent control."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question implies a need to define 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles based on play-by-play and derived play-type data. This will involve mapping specific player actions (e.g., initiator, play type) to these abstract roles.",
    "Separability from talent/counts will require statistical modeling that includes both role coverage metrics and player talent metrics as independent variables, likely using player-level aggregated statistics as talent proxies.",
    "'Talent control behaves' likely refers to the effectiveness and expected direction of the talent control variables in the statistical model, ensuring they account for player quality as anticipated.",
    "Within-team variation implies comparing different lineup compositions and their associated role coverages for the same team across a season or multiple seasons."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definitions and operationalization of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles are not specified and will be critical for the study.",
    "The specific methodology for 'talent control' and what 'behaves' means in this context needs to be clearly defined.",
    "The specific outcome variable (e.g., offensive rating, effective field goal percentage, assist percentage) by which the effect of lineup coverage and its separability from talent will be assessed is not stated."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was returned, so the decision was not influenced by prior work."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The definition and assignment of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles from raw play-by-play data are complex and subjective. The reliability and validity of these role measures are critical and not yet established."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No historical evidence was found for this specific research question or related methodological approaches concerning structural roles (organizer, shaper, terminal) and their separability from talent."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The operationalization of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles from play-by-play data is not trivial. If these roles are poorly defined or measured, the entire analysis will be compromised. This includes how a lineup's 'coverage' is determined from individual player roles."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Separating the effects of role coverage from raw player talent is challenging. Unmeasured aspects of talent, specific player combinations, coaching schemes, and opponent strength could confound the observed effects. Lineup selection is non-random, as coaches make choices based on various factors not fully observable."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "While the total number of possessions is large (420,993), specific lineup compositions with particular role coverages (e.g., exactly one of each role) might be rare, especially when examining within-team variation. This could lead to insufficient data points for robust comparisons and statistical power issues for certain effects."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Lineup changes are often made in response to performance or specific game situations. This non-random selection process can introduce bias, where changes in lineup performance might be attributed to role coverage when they are simply regression to the mean or a response to transient factors."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If the definition of 'organizer', 'shaper', or 'terminal' role is derived too closely from the very play-types or player actions that are also indicative of performance (the implicit outcome), there could be a circularity or mechanical relationship, rather than a truly separable structural effect."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.3,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.3,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of specific lineup structural role coverages (e.g., minimum one organizer, one shaper, one terminal) on offensive and defensive efficiency, after controlling for player talent and other confounding factors, and to quantify the extent of within-team variation in these coverages.",
    "unit": "Possession. Lineups will be defined by the five players on the court at the start of each possession.",
    "sample": "All 420,993 possessions from the NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 regular seasons and playoffs. The 90 team-seasons will be used for within-team variation analysis. Only possessions with valid 5-player lineups will be included. Possessions immediately following substitutions or timeouts will be handled carefully to ensure lineup stability.",
    "power": "Power analysis will be conducted using simulated data based on initial estimates of variance and effect sizes, targeting a minimum of 80% power to detect a 0.05 standard deviation change in offensive/defensive rating due to a shift in role coverage, given the sample size of possessions and expected distribution of role coverages. Sparse lineup combinations will be explicitly noted and potentially excluded if sample sizes are too low.",
    "controls": "Player talent will be controlled using a combination of player-level advanced metrics (e.g., Estimated Plus-Minus, Box Plus/Minus, or similar impact metrics from prior seasons) aggregated to the lineup level (e.g., sum of individual player talent scores). Other controls will include opponent strength (e.g., opponent team rating), game state (score differential, time remaining, quarter), coach identity, and season fixed effects. Team fixed effects will be used to analyze within-team variation. A hierarchical model structure (possessions nested within lineups, nested within teams) will be explored.",
    "falsification": "To assess 'talent control behaves', we will verify that talent control variables (e.g., sum of player EPM) show a statistically significant and expected positive correlation with offensive efficiency and negative correlation with defensive efficiency. Additionally, residual analysis will check for remaining correlations between residuals and talent metrics, indicating effective control. A robustness check will involve using different sets of talent proxies (e.g., raw box score stats vs. advanced metrics).",
    "multiplicity": "Multiple hypothesis testing will be addressed using a False Discovery Rate (FDR) control procedure (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg) if multiple outcomes or role coverage definitions are explored simultaneously. The primary analysis will focus on a pre-defined set of role coverage definitions and one primary outcome (e.g., net rating).",
    "decision_rule": "The research question's components will be evaluated based on statistical significance (p < 0.05) and the practical significance of estimated effect sizes. The existence of within-team variation will be assessed by statistical tests comparing different lineup types within a team. Separability will be supported if role coverage effects remain significant after robust talent controls. 'Talent control behaves' will be supported if talent control variables are statistically significant and in the expected direction, and residual analysis indicates no substantial remaining talent-related confounding.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (complete) regular season and playoffs data from the NBA stats API, including play-by-play, substitution rotations, starters, defensive matchup minutes, box-score summary, and derived play-type possession records."
  }
}

